1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,675
Hi, we are Team Aperture from
Universidad Nacional de Colombia.

2
00:00:04,725 --> 00:00:08,575
Latin America changed its
demography in record time.

3
00:00:08,625 --> 00:00:11,950
Did learning become more
equal along the way?

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:17,204
We use PISA, the OECD test for
fifteen-year-olds in mathematics,

5
00:00:17,254 --> 00:00:22,950
reading and science, with its official
trend tables from 2009 to 2025.

6
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:27,285
Our question: as fertility
and infant mortality fell,

7
00:00:27,335 --> 00:00:32,110
did learning inequality within
each country shrink or grow?

8
00:00:32,160 --> 00:00:36,526
We follow the eight countries
with PISA in every cycle

9
00:00:36,576 --> 00:00:41,351
and measure the gap between
the top and bottom ten percent.

10
00:00:41,401 --> 00:00:44,950
We set these rules before
seeing any result.

11
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:48,773
First, demography. It changed
the same way everywhere:

12
00:00:48,823 --> 00:00:52,950
fertility and infant mortality
fell in all eight countries.

13
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:57,887
But a learning gap can close
for two very different reasons:

14
00:00:57,937 --> 00:01:02,412
upward, when the weakest
students improve, or downward,

15
00:01:02,462 --> 00:01:05,950
when everyone falls
and the best fall more.

16
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:07,954
And here the paths split.

17
00:01:08,004 --> 00:01:13,085
In mathematics, five of eight
countries closed the gap downward.

18
00:01:13,135 --> 00:01:14,769
In science, none did:

19
00:01:14,819 --> 00:01:18,055
four closed it upward and four widened it

20
00:01:18,105 --> 00:01:21,022
because their best students improved.

21
00:01:21,072 --> 00:01:24,950
Only Peru closed it upward
in all three subjects.

22
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:30,135
In mathematics, the top ten
percent fell more than the bottom

23
00:01:30,185 --> 00:01:34,555
in Argentina, Mexico, Uruguay,
Chile and Costa Rica,

24
00:01:34,605 --> 00:01:38,635
and more students ended
below basic proficiency.

25
00:01:38,685 --> 00:01:41,950
Peru is the one that lifted its bottom.

26
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:45,622
Part of this is good news in disguise.

27
00:01:45,672 --> 00:01:51,324
PISA now reaches nine to twenty
points more of each cohort,

28
00:01:51,374 --> 00:01:56,639
so students who used to be
out of school take the test.

29
00:01:56,689 --> 00:02:01,181
Correcting for that halves
the drop at the top,

30
00:02:01,231 --> 00:02:04,950
but in five countries it is still real.

31
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:08,448
And demography does not
explain these paths.

32
00:02:08,498 --> 00:02:14,251
The countries that cut fertility or
infant mortality the most are not the

33
00:02:14,301 --> 00:02:16,477
ones whose gap changed most.

34
00:02:16,527 --> 00:02:20,214
None of the twelve
correlations is significant,

35
00:02:20,264 --> 00:02:23,950
and this holds when we
drop any single country.

36
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:26,317
Peru and Brazil show it best:

37
00:02:26,367 --> 00:02:31,134
almost the same fall in infant
mortality, opposite results.

38
00:02:31,184 --> 00:02:35,950
Peru’s bottom ten percent rose
39 points; Brazil’s fell 10.

39
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:38,865
So Peru deserves a closer look.

40
00:02:38,915 --> 00:02:43,941
Its lowest performers gained
34 points in mathematics,

41
00:02:43,991 --> 00:02:46,950
37 in reading and 47 in science.

42
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:52,584
They rose cycle after cycle until
2018, from 250 to 294 points,

43
00:02:52,634 --> 00:02:57,950
and then stalled, while the rest
of the region barely moved.

44
00:02:58,000 --> 00:02:59,950
So, what did Peru get right?

45
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:02,303
Partly, where it started:

46
00:03:02,353 --> 00:03:07,574
the countries furthest behind
in 2009 improved the most,

47
00:03:07,624 --> 00:03:10,303
and Peru is the extreme case.

48
00:03:10,353 --> 00:03:15,950
We found this while exploring,
so it is a clue, not a proof.

49
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:21,078
Its students’ socioeconomic status
also jumped between 2012 and 2015,

50
00:03:21,128 --> 00:03:23,977
right when its bottom ten percent rose.

51
00:03:24,027 --> 00:03:26,950
But across countries, this link is weak.

52
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:29,429
And Peru changed its schools:

53
00:03:29,479 --> 00:03:32,506
a merit-based teacher career in 2012

54
00:03:32,556 --> 00:03:36,950
and the Full School Day in
secondary school in 2015.

55
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:42,354
Experts credit better teachers,
more class hours and less poverty.

56
00:03:42,404 --> 00:03:45,710
The World Bank says the gains came mainly

57
00:03:45,760 --> 00:03:50,950
from fewer students at the lowest
levels, exactly what we found.

58
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:55,891
Progress stalled after 2018, with
frequent changes of ministers

59
00:03:55,941 --> 00:03:58,950
and almost two years of closed schools.

60
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:02,226
Our answer: same
demography, different paths.

61
00:04:02,276 --> 00:04:05,647
Fertility and infant
mortality fell everywhere,

62
00:04:05,697 --> 00:04:09,360
but learning inequality
followed no common pattern.

63
00:04:09,410 --> 00:04:14,237
And by 2035 the region will have
about twenty percent fewer births.

64
00:04:14,287 --> 00:04:17,950
Fewer students are an
opportunity, not a guarantee.

65
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:20,591
We followed the ASA Ethical Guidelines:

66
00:04:20,641 --> 00:04:25,330
rules set in advance, all three
subjects reported, coverage corrected,

67
00:04:25,380 --> 00:04:28,106
projections shown with their uncertainty,

68
00:04:28,156 --> 00:04:30,950
and no causal claims from eight countries.

69
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:36,950
Peru shows that the gap can be
closed by moving up. Thank you.
